st: the OTel data model, trace corre\n\n\n---\napplyTo: \"**\"\n---\n# The No-Guessing Protocol\n\n*Normative guidance for the single most persistent failure in agent work: **acting on a belief that was never checked**,\n\nNormative keywords (**MUST**, **SHOULD**, **MAY**, **MUST NOT**) follow RFC 2119.\n\nThe governing idea, stated plainly because it is the part that gets missed: **you cannot decide to stop guessing, becaus\n\nSo this protocol does not ask you to detect a guess by feel. It does three mechanical things instead:\n\n1. **Removes the fourth option.** When you don't know, there are exactly three moves — check, mark, ask (NG1). Proceedin\n\n---\n\n## 1. The core rules\n\n**NG1 — Three permitted responses to not-knowing. There is no fourth.**\n\n| Move | When | What it looks like |\n\n**Proceeding on an unmarked belief is not on this list.** If you find yourself doing it, you have not made a decision — \n\n**NG2 — The pre-registration rule.** **An assumption that was not written down before the work is not an assumption — it\n\nThis is the rule that removes the post-hoc excuse. \"I assumed the timestamps were UTC\" is only a legitimate sentence if \n\nCorollary: an artifact's assumptions section is written **at the start**, not reconstructed at the end. An assumption di\n\n**NG3 — Know the tells; they are linguistic before they are logical.** Guessing has a reliable signature. In any stateme\n\n> *should be · presumably · typically · usually · I believe · it looks like · probably · must be · seems to · I'd expect\n\nAnd the structural tells, which are worse because they read as confident:\n\n- naming an API, parameter, flag, method or field **you did not open**;\n\n**Rule:** when a tell appears in a load-bearing claim, you **MUST** either check it (NG1) or convert it into a marked as\n\n**NG4 — The `assume:` marker.** Where a belief cannot be checked now, record it inline in the pack's existing marker idi\n\n```python\n\nA marker without a confirmation route and a consequence is not a marker — it is a shrug with a comment character in fron\n\n**NG5 — Cheapest-check-first: if checking costs less than marking, you may not mark.** Before writing a marker, price th\n\n**NG6 — Confidence labels describe evidence, not confidence.** The Rigor Protocol's ledger is not a feeling scale:\n\n- **Verified** — you *observed* it. You ran it, read it, or cited the authoritative source. Reproducible by someone else\n\n**You may not label a claim Verified because it is very likely.** Probability is not evidence, and a high-probability wr\n\n**NG7 — Never launder a guess through a citation, a tool, or a delegate.** A search result, a sub-agent's inventory, a g\n\n- A **web search** result is a lead until checked against the primary source (BoK §III.1).\n\n**NG8 — Ask when it is consequential *and* unresolvable; never to offload what you can check.** Naming a fork you cannot\n\n**NG9 — Every bug that traces to a guess is a defect-class instance.** When a defect's root cause is \"we acted on an unc\n\n**NG10 — Report the split, always.** A closing summary **MUST** separate what is **Verified**, what remains **Inferred**\n\n**NG11 — Scale with cost-of-error.** This is a discipline, not a ceremony. At **T0** (a rename, a typo, a comment) check\n\n---\n\n## 2. The intervention that actually works at the moment of writing\n\nRules that only fire at review time do not stop the guess that is being written *now*. One question does, and it **SHOUL\n\n> ### **\"If this is wrong, how would I find out — and when?\"**\n\n- *\"The type checker would catch it\"* → fine, proceed.\n\nThe second question, for anything already written down:\n\n> ### **\"What did I write here that I did not actually look at?\"**\n\nRun it over a design document before the gate. Designs are where unchecked claims are cheapest to make and most expensiv\n\n---\n\n## 3. Self-verification checklist\n\n- [ ] Every unknown resolved by one of the **three moves** — checked, marked